- namespace_command

' This is publication between two worlds, as pure rules over the namespace. A
' world publishes the names below a root record, /<self>/our/<peer>. The
' reader holds them at its mount, /<self>/her/<publisher>. The reader keeps
' where the mount stands on a card of the reader. No rule here does I/O. A
' foot carries the epics that these rules make, and it admits the writes that
' they plan as one event.
+ net
  + stamp
    ' This function gives the version that a world publishes: its rift and its
    ' case as one number. A restore increases the rift. Thus a world does not
    ' use a published version again. A newer continuity also compares greater
    ' than an older continuity at all times. As a result, a reference pinned
    ' to a stamp names one content permanently.
    \ rift=nat case=nat
    ^ nat
    | add (mul rift 4294967296) case
  + entry
    ' This is one name of an epic. It has:
    ' - its path below the root
    ' - the version of its record
    ' - the signature of the publisher over that moment (0: unsigned)
    ' - the content hash of the record (0: the name is gone)
    ' - the record
    '
    ' An epic travels as its entries without their records. Each record
    ' follows by its hash. The publisher does not send a record that the
    ' reader already holds.
    : rel=path stamp=nat sig=nat hash=nat record=maybe[myth]
  + put
    ' This function makes an unsigned entry for a record.
    \ rel=path stamp=nat record=myth
    ^ net/entry
    | net/entry rel stamp 0 (net/content record) (./some record)
  + drop
    ' This function makes an entry for a name that is gone.
    \ rel=path
    ^ net/entry
    = absent=maybe[myth] .none
    | net/entry rel 0 0 0 absent
  + epic
    ' This is a consistent change of one published root, from its version base
    ' (0: from no record) to its version head. It has the signature of the
    ' publisher over all of the change (0: unsigned).
    : base=nat head=nat sig=nat entries=row[net/entry]
  + pin
    ' This function gives the name, below a published root, of a content that
    ' a record of that root cites. The name is the content hash. Thus the name
    ' holds one content permanently, in each world that holds it, and a
    ' restore of its publisher cannot change it.
    \ hash=nat
    ^ path
    ['pin ($x hash)]
  + cites
    ' This function gives the pinned references that a record holds to the
    ' records of this world, each with its slot.
    \ self=iota record=myth
    ^ row[[slot=path target=safe]]
    = none=row[[slot=path target=safe]] []
    | record.tap.fold none
      \ out [slot value]
      ? value
       > target=safe
        ? (eq target.ver 0) out
        ? (eq target.path.len 0) out
        ? !(reference_index/segment_is target.path.at(0) self) out
        ? !(?=(.x target.care)) out
        | out.snoc([slot target])
      out
  + cited
    ' This function gives the record that a pinned reference names. It gives
    ' none when this world cannot read the record at that case.
    \ target=safe state=namespace
    ^ maybe[myth]
    = none=maybe[myth] .none
    ?> (./known records) (namespace/page .x (./some target.ver) target.path state) none
    ?> (./some held) records.fil none
    | ./some (unpin held.myth)
  + sent
    ' This function gives a record as its publisher sends it. A case of the
    ' publisher has no meaning in a different world. Thus a pinned reference
    ' to a record of the publisher becomes a pinned reference to the name of
    ' the content of that record (net/pin). The content is itself as its
    ' publisher sends it. The reference then names the same content in each
    ' world. A reference that the publisher cannot read stays as it is.
    \ self=iota root=path record=myth state=namespace
    ^ myth
    | (net/cites self record).fold record
      \ out=myth item=[slot=path target=safe]
      ^ myth
      = [slot target] item
      ?> (./some held) (net/cited target state) out
      = hash (net/content (net/sent self root held state))
      | out.put(slot (safe 1 .x root.weld((net/pin hash))))
  + pins
    ' This function adds an entry for each content that a record cites, and
    ' for each content that those contents cite. The entry has the name of
    ' the content (net/pin) and the version of the record that was cited. A
    ' content that is already in the entries is not added again. Thus a
    ' reader gets each content that it needs to read what it gets, and only a
    ' reader of this root gets it.
    \ self=iota rift=nat root=path record=myth state=namespace out=row[net/entry]
    ^ row[net/entry]
    | (net/cites self record).fold out
      \ acc=row[net/entry] item=[slot=path target=safe]
      ^ row[net/entry]
      = [slot target] item
      ?> (./some held) (net/cited target state) acc
      = body (net/sent self root held state)
        rel=path (net/pin (net/content body))
      ? (acc.any((\ seen=net/entry (equal[str] (path_show seen.rel) (path_show rel))))) acc
      | net/pins self rift root held state acc.snoc((net/put rel (net/stamp rift target.ver) body))
  + mispinned
    ' This function tells if an entry has the name of a content (net/pin) and
    ' is not that content.
    \ item=net/entry
    ^ bool
    ? (eq 0 item.hash) .false
    ? !(eq 2 item.rel.len) .false
    ? !(reference_index/segment_is item.rel.at(0) 'pin) .false
    | !(equal[str] (path_show item.rel) (path_show (net/pin item.hash)))
  + standin
    ' This function identifies a record that stands for another record. Its
    ' one slot, is, holds a pinned reference to one of the names of this
    ' world. The world publishes it as the record of that name at the case of
    ' that name. Thus a reader holds the name at its case, and this world
    ' makes no second record of it.
    \ self=iota record=myth
    ^ maybe[safe]
    = none=maybe[safe] .none
    ? !(eq 1 record.tap.len) none
    ?> (./some held) record.get(['is]) none
    ?> link=safe held none
    ? (eq 0 link.ver) none
    ? (eq 0 link.path.len) none
    ? !(reference_index/segment_is link.path.at(0) self) none
    | ./some link
  + puts
    ' This function gives the names of after that are new or changed from
    ' before. The function enters a name that stands for another name
    ' (net/standin) as the record and the case that it names. If the reference
    ' of such a name no longer reads, the function does not include that name.
    \ rift=nat root=path before=axal[saga] after=axal[saga] state=namespace
    ^ row[net/entry]
    = none=row[net/entry] []
      self state.self
    | after.tap.fold none
      \ out [rel now]
      = case now.aeon.yuga.x.data
        same
          ?> (./some then) before.get(rel) .false
          | eq then.aeon.yuga.x.data case
      ? same out
      = own (unpin now.myth)
      ?> (./some link) (net/standin self own) (net/pins self rift root own state out.snoc((net/put rel (net/stamp rift case) (net/sent self root own state))))
      ?> (./known records) (namespace/page .x (./some link.ver) link.path state) out
      ?> (./some held) records.fil out
      = body (unpin held.myth)
      | net/pins self rift root body state out.snoc((net/put rel (net/stamp rift link.ver) (net/sent self root body state)))
  + gone
    ' This function gives the names of before that after no longer has.
    \ before=axal[saga] after=axal[saga]
    ^ row[net/entry]
    = none=row[net/entry] []
    | before.tap.fold none
      \ out [rel then]
      ? (maybe/is_some after.get(rel)) out
      | out.snoc((net/drop rel))
  + delta
    ' This function gives what changed below a published root between its z
    ' cases k and n. k = 0 asks for all that is below the root. If the
    ' function cannot answer the case k or the case n exactly, it gives none.
    ' The reader then asks for all that is below the root. Thus the function
    ' does not give a wrong or partial answer in the place of an answer that
    ' it does not have.
    \ rift=nat root=path k=nat n=nat state=namespace
    ^ maybe[net/epic]
    ? !(lt k n) .none
    = history state.local
      empty (axal/new[saga] .none)
    ?> (./some now) (namespace/epoch .z (./some n) root history) .none
    = after | namespace/records now root history
    ? (eq k 0)
      | ./some (net/epic 0 (net/stamp rift n) 0 (net/puts rift root empty after state))
    ?> (./some then) (namespace/epoch .z (./some k) root history) .none
    = before | namespace/records then root history
      changed | net/puts rift root before after state
    | ./some (net/epic (net/stamp rift k) (net/stamp rift n) 0 changed.weld((net/gone before after)))
  + content
    ' This function gives the content hash of a record: the hash of its pin.
    \ record=myth
    ^ nat
    | PinHash (pin record)
  + word
    ' This function gives what the signature of a moment is made over. A
    ' moment says: this name of this world, at this version, is this content.
    ' The function hashes it as a plain tuple. Thus the same statement is the
    ' same bytes in each world.
    \ world=iota name=path version=nat hash=nat
    ^ nat
    = said=[w=iota n=path v=nat h=nat] [world name version hash]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + bill
    ' This function gives what an epic lists, without the records: each name,
    ' its version and its content hash. The content hash is 0 when the name is
    ' gone.
    \ entries=row[net/entry]
    ^ row[[rel=path stamp=nat hash=nat]]
    | entries.map
      \ item=net/entry
      ^ [rel=path stamp=nat hash=nat]
      [item.rel item.stamp item.hash]
  + heading
    ' This function gives what the signature of an epic is made over. This is:
    ' whose root it is, the version it starts from, the version it goes to,
    ' and its bill. Thus the publisher also signs removals and the head.
    \ root=path base=nat head=nat entries=row[net/entry]
    ^ nat
    = said=[r=path b=nat h=nat e=row[[rel=path stamp=nat hash=nat]]] [root base head (net/bill entries)]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + seal
    ' This function signs an epic as its publisher: first the moment of each
    ' record, then the whole epic. The seed is the key that the publisher
    ' signs with. The foot of the publisher holds the seed, and the seed is
    ' not a record.
    \ seed=nat root=path epic=net/epic
    ^ net/epic
    = world root.at(0)
      signed
        | epic.entries.map
          \ item=net/entry
          ^ net/entry
          ? (eq 0 item.hash) item
          = said (net/word world root.weld(item.rel) item.stamp item.hash)
          | item.set_sig((Ed25519Sign seed said))
    | net/epic epic.base epic.head (Ed25519Sign seed (net/heading root epic.base epic.head signed)) signed
  + moment
    ' This is what a publisher said about one version of one of its names: the
    ' version (a stamp) and the signature over the moment (0: unsigned).
    : sig=nat version=nat
  + stated
    ' This function tells if the holder of a key said that a name, at a
    ' version, is the content with this hash.
    \ key=nat name=path said=net/moment hash=nat
    ^ bool
    ? (eq 0 said.sig) .false
    | eq 1 (Ed25519Verify key (net/word name.at(0) name said.version hash) said.sig)
  + attested
    ' This function tells if a record, at the version that a statement gives,
    ' is what the holder of a key said of a name. Each one that holds the
    ' record, the statement and the key can examine this. It is not necessary
    ' to ask the publisher.
    \ key=nat name=path said=net/moment record=myth
    ^ bool
    | net/stated key name said (net/content record)
  + proven
    ' This function tells if an epic is from its publisher. It examines the
    ' whole epic and each moment in it. It also compares each record that
    ' arrived with the hash that its moment names. An entry with the name of
    ' a content must be that content.
    \ key=nat root=path epic=net/epic
    ^ bool
    ? !(eq 1 (Ed25519Verify key (net/heading root epic.base epic.head epic.entries) epic.sig)) .false
    | epic.entries.all
      \ item=net/entry
      ^ bool
      ? (eq 0 item.hash) (maybe/is_none item.record)
      ? (net/mispinned item) .false
      ? !(net/stated key root.weld(item.rel) (net/moment item.sig item.stamp) item.hash) .false
      ?> (./some record) item.record .true
      | eq item.hash (net/content record)
  + within
    ' This function gives a path within a world: the same path without the
    ' world that it starts at.
    \ whole=path
    ^ path
    = none=path []
    % i=nat 1
      out=path none
    ^ path
    ? (ge i whole.len) out
    | _ i.inc out.snoc(whole.at(i))
  + held
    ' This function gives a number kept on a record. It gives 0 when there is
    ' none.
    \ slot=path name=path state=namespace
    ^ nat
    | (sovereign_care .x name state).fil.fall(myth/new).nat_at(slot 0)
  + key
    ' This function gives the key that a publisher signs with, as the reader
    ' holds it. The key is on the card of the mount. Or, for a second root of
    ' the same publisher (its public root), it is on the publisher's own card
    ' above that card, /<self>/io/mesa/<publisher>. The function gives 0 when
    ' the reader holds no key: the publisher is then a stranger.
    \ card=path state=namespace
    ^ nat
    = own | net/held ['key] card state
    ? !(eq 0 own) own
    ? (le card.len 4) 0
    | net/held ['key] card.take(4) state
  + forked
    ' This function tells if the reader holds proof that the custody of a
    ' publisher signed two futures (pass/turn). The reader believes no
    ' information about such a name.
    \ card=path state=namespace
    ^ bool
    ? (gt (net/held ['fork] card state) 0) .true
    ? (le card.len 4) .false
    | gt (net/held ['fork] card.take(4) state) 0
  + note
    ' This function gives where this world keeps what a publisher said about
    ' a name of a mount. The mount name /<self>/her/<publisher>/... has its
    ' statement at /<self>/io/mesa/<publisher>/said/...
    '
    ' A statement is a record of this world. It is not a part of the record
    ' that landed, and it is not in the version header of that record. Thus a
    ' landed record has the case that this world gave it, and the statement
    ' has a case of its own. A hand can read the history of the statement to
    ' learn which versions of the publisher this world got.
    \ name=path
    ^ path
    = head=path [name.at(0) 'io 'mesa name.at(2) 'said]
    | head.weld(name.drop(3))
  + stating
    ' This function gives the record of a statement: the version of the
    ' publisher, its signature, and the content hash that it signed. A hash of
    ' 0 says that the publisher no longer has the name.
    \ stamp=nat sig=nat hash=nat
    ^ myth
    | ((myth/sing ['stamp] (pails/n stamp)).put(['sig] (pails/n sig))).put(['hash] (pails/n hash))
  + forget
    ' This function adds the write that says a name of a mount is gone at a
    ' head. It adds no write when this world holds no statement for the name.
    \ target=path head=nat state=namespace out=row[[p=path q=writ]]
    ^ row[[p=path q=writ]]
    ? (eq 0 (net/version target state)) out
    | out.snoc([(net/note target) (writ/make (net/stating head 0 0))])
  + said
    ' This function gives the statement of the publisher on the version of a
    ' name that this world got last. It gives zero when this world holds no
    ' statement for the name, or when the statement says that the name is
    ' gone.
    \ name=path state=namespace
    ^ net/moment
    = none (net/moment 0 0)
    ? (lt name.len 3) none
    = at=path (net/note name)
    ? (eq 0 (net/held ['hash] at state)) none
    | net/moment (net/held ['sig] at state) (net/held ['stamp] at state)
  + version
    ' This function gives the version of the publisher that a held record
    ' stands at. It gives 0 when the record is not held.
    \ name=path state=namespace
    ^ nat
    | (net/said name state).version
  + live
    \ name=path state=namespace
    ^ bool
    | maybe/is_some (sovereign_care .x name state).fil
  + above
    ' This function applies a law of the kernel. The kernel lets no record
    ' live below a name that held a record and now holds none. Within one
    ' continuity, a publisher obeys the same law, and thus this function has
    ' no effect. After a restore, the history of the publisher is a different
    ' history. If that history lands below a name where it has no record and
    ' where this world removed one, the function makes that name again, empty.
    \ mount=path rel=path made=axal[myth] state=namespace out=row[[p=path q=writ]]
    ^ row[[p=path q=writ]]
    % k=nat 0
      acc=row[[p=path q=writ]] out
    ^ row[[p=path q=writ]]
    ? (ge k rel.len) acc
    = name=path rel.take(k)
      target=path mount.weld(name)
    ? (maybe/is_some made.dip(name).fil) (_ k.inc acc)
    ? (net/live target state) (_ k.inc acc)
    ? !(maybe/is_some state.case(.x target)) (_ k.inc acc)
    ? (acc.any((\ [at wit] (equal[path] at target)))) (_ k.inc acc)
    | _ k.inc acc.snoc([target (writ/make myth/new)])
  + land
    ' This function gives the writes that land one epic of its publisher at a
    ' mount, as one event. The writes include the head that the mount then
    ' stands at. Each record lands as an ordinary record of this world, with
    ' the content that the publisher published and a case of this world. The
    ' version and the signature of the publisher go in a record of their own
    ' (net/note).
    '
    ' The card holds the key of the publisher. The function does not accept an
    ' epic from a world whose key the reader does not hold. It does not accept
    ' an epic that this key did not sign. It also does not accept an epic that
    ' is not newer than what the mount holds (a repeat or a regression). And
    ' it does not accept an epic that does not start where the mount stands.
    ' In each of these conditions, the function accepts no part of the epic.
    '
    ' The function does not change a record that the mount holds at its
    ' version or at a later version. It does not remove again a name that is
    ' already gone. Thus no entry lands two times and no version goes back. An
    ' epic of all that is below the root also removes what the publisher no
    ' longer has.
    '
    ' A mount is a copy of one published root. Thus the function does not
    ' write and does not remove a name that the kernel spells into another
    ' root (the public root below a pairwise mount).
    \ mount=path card=path epic=net/epic state=namespace
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    = have | net/held ['head] card state
    ? (le epic.head have) (./err 'net_stale)
    ? !((eq epic.base 0) || (eq epic.base have)) (./err 'net_gap)
    = root=path (reference_index/canonical state.self mount)
      inside (\ rel=path (pith_prefix root (reference_index/canonical state.self mount.weld(rel))))
    ? (epic.entries.any((\ item=net/entry !(inside item.rel)))) (./err 'net_outside)
    = key | net/key card state
    ? (eq 0 key) (./err 'net_stranger)
    ? (net/forked card state) (./err 'net_forked)
    ? !(net/proven key root epic) (./err 'net_forged)
    ? !(net/whole mount epic state) (./err 'net_missing)
    = none=row[[p=path q=writ]] []
      cull=writ .cull
      whole (eq epic.base 0)
      made
        | epic.entries.fold (axal/new[myth] .none)
          \ tree=axal[myth] item=net/entry
          ^ axal[myth]
          ? (eq 0 item.hash) tree
          | tree.put(item.rel myth/new)
      moved
        | epic.entries.fold none
          \ out=row[[p=path q=writ]] item=net/entry
          ^ row[[p=path q=writ]]
          = target=path mount.weld(item.rel)
          ? (eq 0 item.hash)
            ? (net/live target state) (net/forget target epic.head state out.snoc([target cull]))
            out
          ? (le item.stamp (net/version target state)) out
          ?> (./some record) item.record out
          = told=writ (writ/make (net/stating item.stamp item.sig item.hash))
          | out.snoc([target (writ/make record)]).snoc([(net/note target) told])
      stale
        ? !whole none
        | (sovereign_peek mount state).tap.fold none
          \ out [rel record]
          ? !(inside rel) out
          = under made.dip(rel)
          ? (maybe/is_some under.fil) out
          = target=path mount.weld(rel)
            told (net/forget target epic.head state out)
          ? (gt under.kid.size 0) told.snoc([target (writ/make myth/new)])
          | told.snoc([target cull])
      bare
        ? !whole none
        | epic.entries.fold none
          \ out=row[[p=path q=writ]] item=net/entry
          ^ row[[p=path q=writ]]
          ? (eq 0 item.hash) out
          | net/above mount item.rel made state out
      mark=myth (myth/sing ['head] (pails/n epic.head))
      writes moved.weld(stale).weld(bare)
    ? (net/live card state) (./ok writes.snoc([card (writ/poke mark)]))
    | ./ok writes.snoc([card (writ/make mark)])
  + lacks
    ' This function gives the content hashes that an epic names for the
    ' records that this world must still get. These are the records that are
    ' newer than what the mount holds.
    \ mount=path epic=net/epic state=namespace
    ^ row[nat]
    = none=row[nat] []
    | epic.entries.fold none
      \ out=row[nat] item=net/entry
      ^ row[nat]
      ? (eq 0 item.hash) out
      ? (maybe/is_some item.record) out
      ? (le item.stamp (net/version mount.weld(item.rel) state)) out
      ? (out.any((\ seen=nat (eq seen item.hash)))) out
      | out.snoc(item.hash)
  + whole
    ' This function tells if each record that an epic will land arrived.
    \ mount=path epic=net/epic state=namespace
    ^ bool
    | eq 0 (net/lacks mount epic state).len
  + given
    ' This function gives an epic with a record that arrived. It puts the
    ' record where the epic names the hash of the record.
    \ hash=nat record=myth epic=net/epic
    ^ net/epic
    | epic.set_entries
      | epic.entries.map
        \ item=net/entry
        ^ net/entry
        ? !(eq item.hash hash) item
        | item.set_record((./some record))
  + bare
    ' This function gives an epic as it travels: its entries without their
    ' records.
    \ epic=net/epic
    ^ net/epic
    = absent=maybe[myth] .none
    | epic.set_entries
      | epic.entries.map
        \ item=net/entry
        ^ net/entry
        | item.set_record(absent)
  + wired
    ' This function gives an epic as it travels: plain rows and no records.
    ' Thus the bytes are the same for each kernel that makes it. Each line is
    ' one name: its path, its version, the signature over that moment, and its
    ' content hash.
    \ epic=net/epic
    ^ [base=nat head=nat sig=nat lines=row[[rel=path stamp=nat sig=nat hash=nat]]]
    = lines
        | epic.entries.map
          \ item=net/entry
          ^ [rel=path stamp=nat sig=nat hash=nat]
          [item.rel item.stamp item.sig item.hash]
    [epic.base epic.head epic.sig lines]
  + read
    ' This function gives an epic from a value that arrived. It makes no
    ' assumption about the value. It gives none unless the value is the form
    ' that an epic has when it travels, to the last nat.
    \ value=any
    ^ maybe[net/epic]
    = none=maybe[net/epic] .none
      absent=maybe[myth] .none
    ?> (./some sent) (fit[[base=nat head=nat sig=nat lines=row[[rel=path stamp=nat sig=nat hash=nat]]]] value) none
    = [base head signed lines] sent
      entries
        | lines.map
          \ [rel stamp sig hash]
          ^ net/entry
          | net/entry rel stamp sig hash absent
    | ./some (net/epic base head signed entries)
  + shaped
    ' This function gives the number of slots of a well-formed myth whose
    ' slots are all between two bounds. It gives none when the tree is not a
    ' tree that the kernel can make. Such a tree has a slot out of order, or a
    ' count that is not the correct sum.
    \ lo=maybe[path] hi=maybe[path] tree=mop[path pail lt_pith]
    ^ maybe[nat]
    > n=mop/node[path pail lt_pith] tree
     | ./some 0
    = none=maybe[nat] .none
      mid=maybe[path] (./some n.key)
      below
        ?> (./some bound) lo .true
        | lt_pith bound n.key
      above
        ?> (./some bound) hi .true
        | lt_pith n.key bound
    ? !(and below above) none
    ?> (./some left) (net/shaped lo mid n.l) none
    ?> (./some right) (net/shaped mid hi n.r) none
    ? !(eq n.sz left.inc.add(right)) none
    | ./some n.sz
  + sound
    ' This function gives a record from a value that arrived. It makes no
    ' assumption about the value:
    ' - It examines the representation of the value at each depth.
    ' - The slots of the value must be in order.
    ' - A slot that the kernel reads as a reference must be a whole reference.
    '
    ' The reader of another slot must examine what the value of that slot
    ' holds. No value that arrives holds code (the runtime does not accept a
    ' stream with a law in it).
    \ value=any
    ^ maybe[myth]
    = none=maybe[myth] .none
      open=maybe[path] .none
    ?> (./some record) (fit[myth] value) none
    ? (maybe/is_none (net/shaped open open record)) none
    = whole
        | record.tap.all
          \ entry
          ?> target=safe entry.val .true
          | maybe/is_some (fit[safe] entry.val)
    ? whole (./some record)
    none
  + shown
    ' This is a record shown to a world that its publisher did not publish it
    ' to. It has the name of the record as its publisher spells it, the
    ' statement of the publisher on that version, and the record. It is an
    ' ordinary value, and thus it travels in each record that shows it. Each
    ' one that holds the key of the publisher can examine it. It is not
    ' necessary to ask the publisher, or the one that showed it.
    : name=path said=net/moment record=pin[myth]
  + show
    ' This function gives what this world holds of a name of a publisher, as a
    ' value to show. It gives none when this world does not hold the name,
    ' when it holds no signed statement of the publisher for the name, or when
    ' the record is not the content that the statement names.
    \ name=path state=namespace
    ^ maybe[net/shown]
    = none=maybe[net/shown] .none
    ?> (./known records) (namespace/page .x .none name state) none
    ?> (./some held) records.fil none
    = said (net/said name state)
    ? (eq 0 said.sig) none
    ? !(eq (PinHash held.myth) (net/held ['hash] (net/note name) state)) none
    | ./some (net/shown (reference_index/canonical state.self name) said held.myth)
  + seen
    ' This function gives the record that a value shows, when the value is a
    ' shown record and the holder of a key said it. The function makes no
    ' assumption about the value.
    \ key=nat value=any
    ^ maybe[net/shown]
    = none=maybe[net/shown] .none
    ?> (./some item) (fit[net/shown] value) none
    ? !(net/attested key item.name item.said (unpin item.record)) none
    | ./some item
  + refuse
    ' This function records a landing that the kernel did not accept. It keeps
    ' the head and the reason in the place that keeps where the mount stands.
    ' The mount stays exactly as it was, and a later epic lands from where the
    ' mount stands.
    \ card=path head=nat why=iota state=namespace
    ^ row[[p=path q=writ]]
    = note=myth (myth/sing ['refused] (pails/n head)).put(['why] (pails/q why))
    ? (net/live card state) [[card (writ/poke note)]]
    | [[card (writ/make note)]]
  + wants
    ' This function tells if the reader must ask for a head that a publisher
    ' gives in a hint. The reader asks only for a head that is later than what
    ' it holds and later than what was not accepted. The reader compares a
    ' hint and does not believe it.
    \ card=path head=nat state=namespace
    ^ bool
    ? (le head (net/held ['head] card state)) .false
    | gt head (net/held ['refused] card state)
  + serving
    ' This function tells if a world can serve. A world can serve only while
    ' it holds all that it announced. mark is the highest epoch that the world
    ' announced. It is kept outside the saved world and compared at boot. If
    ' the world is behind mark, the world was restored. Then its rift
    ' increases before the world publishes one version again.
    '
    ' A foot is told of an epoch only after the store saves that epoch. Thus a
    ' crash alone does not lose an epoch.
    \ mark=nat state=namespace
    ^ bool
    | ge state.local.top mark
  + ahead
    ' This function tells if a reader asks from a version that this world did
    ' not make until now. A reader asks from the version that it holds. If
    ' this world did not make that version until now, a tree that this world
    ' no longer has made it.
    \ rift=nat root=path from=nat state=namespace
    ^ bool
    | gt from (net/stamp rift state.case(.z root).fall(0))
